      claude: `Act as an AI architect. Explain the retrieval-grounding pattern: why models cannot know your private content, the retrieve-then-generate flow (chunk, embed, search, inject, answer with citations), why retrieval quality dominates model quality, the honesty instruction (say when it is not in the sources), and the small-corpus shortcut of pasting everything. Under 18 lines.`,
      chatgpt: `I want a chatbot that answers questions about my own documentation. How do I make the AI know about documents it was never trained on? Everyone says 'RAG' but I don't know what that involves - do I need a vector database? Can I start simpler with 30 pages of docs? And how do I stop it making up answers that sound right?`,
      lovable: `Build a grounded question and answer feature over this content: retrieve the most relevant sections for each question, generate the answer from those sections only, display the supporting sources with each claim, and return an honest not-in-the-sources response when retrieval finds nothing relevant.`,
      generic: `Explain retrieval-grounding (RAG): retrieving relevant chunks of your own content and injecting them into the prompt so the model answers from evidence rather than memory, chunking and metadata quality as the dominant factor, citation and honesty instructions, the paste-everything shortcut for small corpora, and when grounding is unnecessary.`,
